# Minimum-viable comments and tests

Both comments and tests are cheap to generate and expensive to maintain. Push back on the default to write more.

## Comments: default to none

- Put reasoning (why this number or shape) in `docs/design.md`, not in code.
- Do not write dates, version numbers or "after pass X" notes. Git records history.
- Do not point at other files ("see `foo.ts`", "matches `BAR`"). They go stale when files move.
- Do not narrate the obvious. If code is unclear, rename or restructure it.
- Keep a comment only for non-obvious intent, a trade-off the code cannot show, or an invariant a reader would miss. Example: "Sort first so the old deck order leaves no trace."

When in doubt, delete the comment and see if anyone notices.

## Tests: follow the cost of a regression

- Test behaviour a player or another module depends on: rules, the engine's move results, undo, bot determinism.
- Test formulas only where the math is not obvious.
- Do not test something the type system already guarantees.
- Do not assert that a constant equals its own literal. Test the function that computes it.
- Prefer one parameterised test (`it.each`) over many near-copies.
- Use one randomised check with bounds instead of several fixed sweeps for the same property.
- A new test should be able to fail. For a fix, check that the test fails without the fix.

When in doubt, delete the test and see if a real regression gets through.
